WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:07.460
Let us begin with a scenario.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:13.030
You are standing next to a six-year-old boy
in Pormpuraaw, an isolated Aboriginal community

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:14.360
in Australia.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.320
When you ask him to point north, he points
precisely and without hesitation.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.010
Your compass says he’s right.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:22.570
You then ask the same question to a group
of your teachers.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:25.730
Not all of them know the answer 
and the ones who think they do

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:30.619
end up pointing in several different directions before eventually reaching a state of confusion.

00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:35.610
Why is it that a six-year-old boy in one culture 
can do something effortlessly that educated,

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:39.020
experienced adults in another culture 
struggle to do?

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:43.900
The surprising answer, as Lera Boroditsky reported in the Wall Street Journal in 2010,

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.340
might be the substantial influence of 
our native language

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:51.980
over our thought processes. I will be using some of her ideas to help me make my points.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:56.400
The relationship between language and thought
has provided a rich topic for anthropological,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.530
psychological and linguistic research.

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:03.859
Language, like culture, is a system we all
create and define together, but unlike culture

00:01:03.859 --> 00:01:05.970
it is tightly structured.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:09.799
The idea that language might shape thought
has often been seen as crazy and irrational,

00:01:09.799 --> 00:01:11.490
and untestable at best.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:16.420
However, a new flurry of cognitive scientific
research has caused a shift in consensus and

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:20.420
led us to wonder: Do the languages we speak 
have an effect on the way in which

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:24.120
we construct our individual perceptions 
of reality and how we see the world?

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.680
Consider a snippet from one of the most well-known
nursery rhymes in the English-speaking world;

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.220
“Humpty Dumpty sat on a wall”, from this one line alone, we can reveal so many

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:36.760
of the differences between languages.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:40.180
If you were singing "Humpty Dumpty" in English, you would have to mark the verb

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.140
for tense and say “sat” rather than “sit”.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:43.600
to indicate timing.

00:01:43.619 --> 00:01:47.560
But in Indonesian you don’t need to, and in
fact- you can't, change the verb to mark

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:48.460
tense.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.300
In Russian, the word that you would use
 would reveal the gender of your protagonist.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:56.220
So your verb would be slightly different
if it was Mr. Dumpty instead of Mrs. Dumpty doing

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:57.140
the sitting.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.960
Lastly, in Turkish, you would have to 
include in your verb how you obtained

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.160
the information that you were talking about. 
So you would use a slightly

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:07.180
different word if you saw Humpty Dumpty
 sitting with your own eyes

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:08.800
than if you read or heard about it.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:14.520
So, as we can see each language requires 
very different things from its speakers.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:18.720
But does this mean that speakers of English, Turkish, Russian and Indonesian end up

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.040
comprehending their experiences differently just because they speak different languages?

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.940
Let’s do another one.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:29.000
Suppose I want to tell you that I saw Uncle
Mike on 47th street.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.700
In Mian, which is a language spoken in Papua New Guinea, the verb that I would use would reveal whether the event

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:37.560
happened just now, yesterday or a longer time ago, whereas in Indonesian it wouldn't

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.600
even reveal if it was still going on.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:42.980
In Russian, the verb would reveal my gender.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.240
And in Mandarin Chinese, I would have 
to specify in my word choice for "uncle"

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.860
whether my uncle is maternal or paternal

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:53.100
and whether he is related by blood or by marriage,
because there are different types of words for all

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.420
these types of uncles.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:59.240
But while the rules of all those languages
seem to add specific information to the content of

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:02.940
my sentences, some languages actually remove
information all together.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:07.400
In Pirahã, which is a language spoken 
in the Amazon by a tribe of the same name,

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:09.280
I couldn’t say 47th because there

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.120
Because the Pirahã have no words for numbers.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.880
In fact, in this way and many others, Pirahã departs from what were long thought

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.360
to be essential features for all languages.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:21.160
As Rafaela von Bredow reported in Der Spiegel,

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.620
The Pirahã use only three pronouns.

00:03:23.629 --> 00:03:27.670
They hardly use any words associated with
time or color, and past verb conjugations

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:28.670
don't exist.

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:32.670
But what has perflexed linguists the most is
that the Pirahã appear to have no words for

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:33.670
numbers.

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:36.129
They do not use words such as “few” or “many”.

00:03:36.129 --> 00:03:39.670
There is one word, “hói”, which comes
close to the numeral 1.

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:42.920
However “hói” can also be used 
to describe relatively small quantities,

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.060
such as two small fish as opposed to one big fish.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:51.110
Peter Gordon, a psycholinguist at Columbia
University, and Daniel Everett from Bentley

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:54.870
University visited the Pirahãs attempting to
test their mathematical abilities.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:57.940
They were asked to repeat patterns
made between 1 and 10

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:01.519
or to remember whether Gordon 
had placed 5 or 8 objects in a can.

00:04:01.519 --> 00:04:06.319
The results were fascinating; the Pirahãs
simply could not grasp the concept of numbers.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:11.640
As Everett stated as a conclusion to his research,
“a people without terms for numbers does

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:14.640
not develop the ability to 
determine exact numbers”.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.200
Which compels us to deduce, that people are
only capable of constructing thoughts for

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:24.200
which they possess actual words, and ultimately,
that language is not just a way for us

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:25.760
to express our thoughts;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.620
it actually plays a substantial
role in creating them.

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:32.700
It is not that the Pirahãs are not intelligent
enough to grasp mathematical concepts;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:37.320
This anomaly does not lie on their
mental capacities being lower than average.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.520
It lies on the origins and purpose
of their language.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:44.729
All languages are created and moulded by the
culture of their speakers, and Pirahã is no

00:04:44.729 --> 00:04:45.729
different.

00:04:45.729 --> 00:04:49.180
Everett explains the cultural core of this
very language with one simple formula

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:51.660
“Live here and live now.”

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:54.560
The only thing of importance that is worth
communicating to others is what

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.000
is being experienced in that very moment:
and that's why all experience

00:04:58.000 --> 00:04:59.160
is anchored in the present.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.640
This also explains why the Pirahã appear to have no

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:04.699
creation myth explaining their existence.

00:05:04.699 --> 00:05:09.330
When asked about it, they simply replied: "Everything
is the same, things always are."

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:13.960
It is the employment of this carpe-diem culture
that restricts abstract thought and complicated

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:16.920
connections to the past, 
limiting the language accordingly.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:22.600
Now, while we explore Pirahã as an example of
how language can limit cognition, the Kuuk Thaayorre

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:28.180
language spoken in this region of Pormpuraaw
actually serves as an example of language improving it.

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:31.560
Unlike English, Kuuk Thaayorre language spoken in Pormpuraaw does not employ

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:33.800
relative spatial terms such as left and right.

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:38.400
Kuuk Thaayorre people actually talk in terms
of absolute cardinal directions, that is,

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:39.940
North, South, East, West.

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:45.000
In English, we also use those terms, but
only for large spatial scales, but in Kuuk

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.020
Thaayore they're used 
at all scales.

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:52.220
So what this means is that in Pormpuraaw, 
one might say “the fork is southeast to the knife”,

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:54.500
or “the boy standing to the west 
of Julie is my brother”.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:06:00.220
As a result, people who speak such languages that
employ absolute cardinal directions are remarkably

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:02.120
good at keeping track of where they are,

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:04.760
because they need to this just 
to be able to speak properly.

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:09.280
It is the requirements of their language that
enforce and train this cognitive prowess.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:11.199
Research has also shown that language

00:06:11.199 --> 00:06:13.650
can influence how we perceive
and understand the concept of time.

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:17.720
Alice Gaby from the University of 
California, Berkeley, gave speakers

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:21.699
of different languages a set of cards showing temporal progressions; such as a man ageing,

00:06:21.699 --> 00:06:24.009
or a crocodile growing.

00:06:24.009 --> 00:06:27.000
The speakers were then asked to take the shuffled 
photographs and arrange them in

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:28.980
correct temporal order.

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:33.020
English speakers given this task will arrange
the cards so that time proceeds from left

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:34.180
to right.

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:38.200
But Hebrew speakers who write from right to left, will tend to arrange the cards so that time proceeds

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:39.200
from right to left.

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:43.300
Which shows us that writing direction
in a language can actually influence how we perceive

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:45.220
and understand the concept of time.

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:48.740
For example, in English the
future is said to be in front of us and

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:50.700
the past is said to be behind us.

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:54.340
And in a study carried out by Lynden Miles, it was
proven that speakers of English will tend to

00:06:54.349 --> 00:06:58.110
sway their bodies forward when discussing
the future and backward when discussing about

00:06:58.110 --> 00:06:59.330
the past.

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:03.960
But in Aymara, a language spoken in the Andes,
the past is said to be in front of us and the future

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:09.480
behind us, and similarly, as proven by 
Eve Sweetser, speakers of Aymara

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:14.560
will tend to sway their bodies forward when discussing the past and backward when discussing the future.

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:18.410
But even more incredible is that language
can go beyond affecting our own abilities

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:22.699
and conceptions, to empower us to
mould how an event appears and influence how

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:24.000
others experience it.

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:27.640
Lera Boroditsky outlines this with one simple example.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:32.020
Consider former vice-president Dick
Cheney’s quail-hunting accident, in which

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:34.420
Cheney accidentally shot Harry Whittington.

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:40.580
In this case, one could say that “Cheney shot Whittington” identifying Cheney as the agent of the event.

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:45.740
One could also say “Whittington was shot by
Cheney” still indicating Cheney as the agent,

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:47.470
but distancing him from the outcome.

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:52.860
Lastly, one could say “Whittington got
shot”, leaving Cheney out altogether.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:56.789
Cheney himself said, “Ultimately I’m the
guy who pulled the trigger that fired the

00:07:56.789 --> 00:08:02.669
round that hit Harry,” interposing a long
chain of events between himself and the outcome.

00:08:02.669 --> 00:08:06.830
President George Bush said— "he turned around, 
and he heard a bird flush, and he pulled the

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:09.360
trigger and saw his friend get wounded”—
transforming

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:12.500
Cheney from agent to mere witness 
in less than a sentence.

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:17.880
These are all just a few of the many fascinating
findings of linguistic differences in cognition.

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:22.560
The languages we speak not only help us express our
thoughts, but actually shape the very thoughts

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:24.200
we wish to express.

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:29.020
The patterns that our languages are made
of immensely shape how we construct our individual perceptions

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:34.240
of reality, build our opinions and interpret
the incredibly complex knowledge systems

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:35.340
that we have.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:36.580
Thank you.

